1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,885
This is the hotel group's tool server, inside the official MCP
Inspector,

2
00:00:08,735 --> 00:00:10,847
a standard client anyone can use.

3
00:00:11,697 --> 00:00:15,068
I'm connecting with a guest's key, and all the data is made up.

4
00:00:15,672 --> 00:00:17,187
A guest sees four tools.

5
00:00:18,037 --> 00:00:21,194
Front-desk staff would see five, and a manager six.

6
00:00:22,044 --> 00:00:24,391
The role decides which tools even exist.

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,968
I'll ask the policy search whether I can bring my dog to the beach
resort.

8
00:00:29,818 --> 00:00:32,037
It returns the passages with their source,

9
00:00:32,887 --> 00:00:36,513
and a note that the text is reference data, never instructions.

10
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:39,124
Now I'll try someone else's booking.

11
00:00:39,974 --> 00:00:41,830
The server says it doesn't exist,

12
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:45,091
so a guest can't even find out that it's there.

13
00:00:45,687 --> 00:00:46,988
My own booking works,

14
00:00:47,838 --> 00:00:50,526
and it shows only what a guest is allowed to see.

15
00:00:51,376 --> 00:00:53,787
No name, no email, no phone number.

16
00:00:54,390 --> 00:00:57,995
Every call is checked against the role and written to an audit log.

17
00:00:58,845 --> 00:01:03,539
In the eval, ninety-nine out of ninety-nine checks pass, across three
runs.

18
00:01:04,389 --> 00:01:07,525
And the server calls no model, so a call costs nothing.

19
00:01:08,125 --> 00:01:10,621
The code is on GitHub, and the link's below.
